  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure slowly pulls away today, resulting\nin lingering unsettled weather. \n\nA vigorous upper-level trough continues to evolve across the \nEastern U.S. today. This supported the surface low deepening \nnear our CWA, which will head NE across the waters off New \nEngland today. Overall, it will be unsettled across our region \ntoday.\n\nNot much has changed with regards to the cold air following the\nsystem and the subsequent change in p-type for NW areas. The \nstrength of the parent upper-level trough results in a colder \nair mass moving in with time, however the initial air mass is \nnot all that cold (the lack of a cold antecedent air mass). As \nthe surface low is consolidating off the northern Mid-Atlantic \ncoast, the winds are turning more out of the northwest and this\nwill increase the cold air advection. This will result in some \ncolder air seeping southeastward today and tonight. The air \nmass has become sufficiently cold enough mostly across our far \nnorthwestern zones for some snow across the higher elevations, \nconfined mainly to the Pocono Plateau. Wouldn't be shocked if \nsome higher elevations got 1-2 inches, but overall less than an \ninch is expected. Some locations NW of I-95, especially higher \nelevations, may see a few wet flakes mixing in, especially as \nprecip winds down late today and tonight, but no accumulations \nare expected. Otherwise, a rain event is forecast. Additional \nrainfall through tonight looks to be maximized along a band from\nnear Allentown southeast into central NJ, where a half inch or \nmore may yet fall. Amounts look to quickly diminish toward the \nsouthwest, with little if any additional rain across \nnortheastern Maryland and much of Delaware.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stretch of below normal temperatures begins \ntoday, continuing through the weekend as a much colder airmass \nmoves overhead.\n\nIn the wake of the storm, as it deepens and tracks toward the \nCanadian Maritimes over the weekend, a colder air mass will be \nin place across our area. In addition, a tightened pressure \ngradient coupled with deeper mixing will result in a period of \nbreezy to windy conditions over the weekend. The strongest winds\nlook to occur on Saturday, with peak gusts to about 30 mph, \nthen while still gusty on Sunday it appears the speeds come down\na little. Daytime temperatures today and Saturday should not \nget out of the 40s for many areas, with temperatures at night \ndropping into the 20s to 30s. There could even be some lake \neffect snow and/or rain showers into the southern Poconos on \nSaturday.\n\nTemperatures start to moderate a bit Sunday through Tuesday. \nDespite that, high temperatures are only in the upper 40s to low\n50s each day which is still below average for this time of \nyear.\n\nWhile we are likely to get our first freeze sometime during \nthis stretch, the Frost/Freeze program area-wide ends on Sunday \nNovember 15th. Beyond that, no further frost/freeze headlines \nwill be issued.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Widespread minor and localized moderate tidal \nflooding expected along the New Jersey coast today.\n\nStrong easterly flow with a deepening area of low pressure\nresulted in a push of water towards the New Jersey coast. Winds\nhave turned more northerly, though the direction overall will \nnot be west enough to drain the water quickly. As a result, \nwidespread minor tidal flooding is expected with this morning's\nhigh tide along the Raritan Bay and New Jersey shore. A Coastal\nFlood Advisory was issued. Higher water levels look possible\nalong Ocean County and its back bays, so this area has been\nupgraded to a Coastal Flood Warning.\n\nFlow has turned more westerly around Delaware Bay, southern\nDelaware, and Chesapeake Bay. As a result, only spotty minor\ntidal flooding at most is expected. \n\nTidal issues are anticipated to diminish after this morning's \nhigh tide.",
